-- Named rejection: a lambda outside a recognised combinator position.
--
-- First-order Horn clauses cannot carry a function as a value. M1 admits a
-- lambda only where an aggregate recogniser consumes it; anywhere else it is
-- LEHigherOrder, not a silently defunctionalised approximation.
--
-- Two definitions, so the batch is visible: `adder` returns a function, and
-- `doubled` uses `map` in a position no aggregate consumes. The second is the
-- one worth reading — `map` IS admitted, but only directly inside an aggregate
-- (`sum (map … xs)` in ../scores.l4), where its lambda becomes the findall
-- template. Standing alone it would have to produce a list value, which is a
-- higher-order transformation this fragment does not lower. It is rejected BY
-- NAME rather than falling through to "unbound reference", because a
-- scope-resolution diagnosis for a fragment decision sends the reader to the
-- wrong place entirely.

IMPORT prelude

@export
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
`adder` n MEANS GIVEN x YIELD x PLUS n

@export
GIVEN xs IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A LIST OF NUMBER
`doubled` xs MEANS map (GIVEN x YIELD x TIMES 2) xs

-- The third is about the DIAGNOSTIC rather than the decision. `all` is a
-- prelude list combinator M1 does not recognise, and it used to fall through to
-- "unbound reference `all` (prelude combinators … are outside the M1 fragment)"
-- — prose that names the fragment under a KIND that says the name did not
-- resolve. #258 §6.1's rejection census counts by kind precisely so it cannot be
-- polluted by prose, so a fragment decision has to be reported as one.
@export
GIVEN xs IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `every positive` IF all (GIVEN x YIELD x GREATER THAN 0) xs
